[Music]  despite trying to avoid it as much as  possible the witcher is filled with cgi  from entire cities to prosthetics to the  magical elements we'll be taking a look  at it all check it out i believe there  are 133 shots in that sequence and 132  of them involve vfx number one you gotta  do what you gotta do  this is the moment before we meet our  friends the basilisks as they appear  through a portal while most of the scene  leading up to this shot isn't cgi it's  quite understandable why a green screen  may have come in handy  trying to be all stoic and serious and  then there's a guy in in green spandex  number two here come the terrifying lime  green lizard heads you've really got to  be willing to pretend as an actor  sometimes especially in the fantasy  genre you know it's going to look  awesome in the end but honestly  sometimes it's hard to take your job  seriously when you're trying to be all  stoic and serious and your acting  partner is supposed to be a scary  basilisk but instead it's just a bright  green head on a stick operated by a guy  in spandex  i sometimes would giggle but with  imagination on their side they manage i  love the illusion world i love to  visualize everything number three if we  turned people into trees we could solve  climate change think about it paul  bullion took on the role of aleshia in  season 2. the entirety of the leshi's  face is all prosthetics which is pretty  sweet we can't imagine how long that  took to apply every day all of the vines  growing off of his head though is where  the cgi magic comes in and who is geralt  attacking  that doesn't look like a leshi to us  nope just a guy flinging around a pool  noodle  okay it's probably not a pool noodle  before we started filming we had to do  quite a bit of training number four the  grass is always green screened somewhere  else we spotted some green screens in  the training scenes too trying to block  out their not so perfect location to  replace it with their mountaintop  destination later on  also is it just us or are the green  ropes new they must be way easier to  edit out in post than the black ones  and if you look closely freya allen  really isn't dodging a thing  i love all the stunt stuff it's so much  fun  number five beautifying the beast you  might be surprised to find out that the  creation of nivalin required little cgi  he was dressed in full costume the only  cgi was his face and i have like tons of  dots and and a camera all the time but  christopher hibiew absolutely loves that  his eyes are his own how they made the  eyes look like mine is just beautiful  work so why the cgi and not just  prosthetics have you said that a reason  is that often beasts don't display  complex emotion but he needed to  justified and it looked great  number six two halves is more effective  than one this scene from episode one of  the witcher is probably a personal fave  it isn't often an actor does all of his  own stunts and check out how he's only  got half a sword that's how he managed  to do some of these cool more dangerous  maneuvers in one shot it allows for a  lot more moves although the other actors  did find it a bit of a struggle because  they needed to match their weapons with  the invisible half of the sword  perfectly number seven if the sword  doesn't work try hitting him with a shoe  vladimir furtick who played the night  king in game of thrones choreographed  the fight between geralt and the  kikimora he acted out the scenes he was  choreographing by standing four people  on a table and giving them each a pair  of pipes to act as legs animating two  legs is hard enough let alone eight they  had to watch the footage at a quarter  speed to make sure it was moving  properly julian perry confessed it was  tricky to keep the creature balanced and  attacking at the same time and who could  forget all that splashing that's all cgi  too  number eight this next one's pretty hot  magic is definitely one of those  elements that is hard to create without  special effects and a lot of cgi tricks  went into shooting this scene the  working skin trick is done using a whole  bunch of different mask-like layers in  different stages of decay and just  blending them all together it makes  total sense but how come we never  thought of that before and check out all  of the work that goes into making the  fireballs  and how about how they're launching  people across the room  why does that look like so much fun  number nine the creature that feeds on  nightmares did the striga creep you out  as much as it did us while the entire  costume was prosthetics including the  dangly umbilical cord overall the  character required a blend of cgi too  they completely redesigned the character  out of special effects for some of the  more  inhuman stunts  we'd say it's impressive but we know  your trick cgi designers you have your  character hide in the darkness to make  it easier to blend the cgi with the real  effects  and the actors were having a blast  they had three artists playing the role  becky wright played the human form but  they had two other performers playing  the creature number 10 we actually  preferred the little guys with their  green suits showing to our surprise the  ghouls were not totally made of cgi the  proof is in the selfies wholesome  content alert oh how cute are these  boy does that suit look uncomfortable  though and eating looks like a major  struggle they were running out of time  to craft these scenes which is why they  landed on a halfway point between  prosthetics and special effects number  11. literal world building it's actually  amazing the kind of work that goes into  creating the buildings for fantasy  series remember how much time you would  spend building your dream house in the  sims or even a small community  this is like next level sims right here  number 12 ironically nothing about this  nature is real  layer after layer after layer added on  painstakingly to bring this forest to  life not even geralt and roach are real  here just leads you to wonder is  anything real just kidding and like  let's talk about posada that too was  entirely modeled from scratch vfx  supervisor julian perry said almost  every minute of this series is a visual  treat which means it required you  guessed it cgi  as finding that perfect location was a  struggle given they were filming in  budapest in the middle of winter  there's no cutting corners with crafting  these cgi bad boys  now these guys seem like they're a  little  lacking in the detail department but we  still like them well enough what they  lack in cgi they sure do make up in  storytelling layers it's all layers  bone structure muscle skin  they definitely weren't skipping steps  when they created him except for maybe  the scales and all these layers remind  us of a line from shrek onions have  layers ogres have layers well dragons  have layers too and lots of them  number fourteen we're gonna need some  cgi sleeves to go with these cgi armies  only about 30 or so people were real out  of this entire army do you think they  craft every tiny person by hand or do  you think they have a copy paste button  drawing a picture is hard enough let  alone layer after layer of designing  creatures and buildings we'll stick to  pencil crayons and stick figures thank  you very much what effect did you find  most interesting let us know in the  comments and thanks for watching

